    thinking: "**Considering SVG Implementation**\n\nOkay, the image generation tool\'s quota got maxed out, which initially threw a wrench in things. However, I\'ve pivoted! I\'m now thinking about implementing the logo as a scalable vector graphic (SVG) within the React components themselves. An SVG would be a far superior choice for web applications, offering lightweight design, perfect scaling, and crisp rendering.\n\n\n**Designing the SVG Component**\n\nI\'m now diving into the SVG implementation! My thoughts have crystallized around designing a `TurtleRockLogo.tsx` component. The plan is to create an SVG with an atomic background of rotating ellipses, six neon green dots, a teal turtle, and a glass dome with bubbling green fluid. I\'m focusing on the curved text for \"TURTLE ROCK\" and \"science club\" and figuring out how to curve the text using `<textPath>` within the SVG.\n\n\n**Refining Curved Text Placement**\n\nI\'m now iterating on the SVG text placement. I\'ve been experimenting with the `textPath` element, adjusting offsets and anchors to get \"TURTLE ROCK\" positioned correctly along the top curve. I\'ve also focused on differentiating the bottom curve and text for \"science club\" -- lowercase, and playful! I\'m now fine-tuning the bottom arc\'s starting point and radius for better visual flow.\n\n\n**Analyzing Path Directives**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the SVG path directives, particularly for the curved text. I\'ve been experimenting with the `A` arc command and how the sweep-flag and radii influence the curve direction, especially when ensuring the text on the bottom curve reads left-to-right. I\'m carefully testing path coordinates. I\'m focusing on the best way to represent the turtle and beaker within the SVG using composed SVG elements.\n\n\n\n**Constructing Ellipse Paths**\n\nI\'m now generating SVG paths. I\'ve defined ellipses with stroke color `#94a3b8`, stroke-width `4`, and fill `none`. I\'m starting with a base ellipse and rotating it, creating three overlapping ellipses with transforms of `0`, `60`, and `120` degrees. Next, I\'m mapping nodes to the ellipse vertices, planning to add glowing spheres at the ends, with color `#4ade80`, radius 6, and stroke `black`.\n\n\n**Optimizing Ellipse Grouping**\n\nI\'ve made a breakthrough with the rotating ellipses! Instead of calculating individual coordinates, I\'m now using SVG `<g>` elements with `transform=\"rotate(angle cx cy)\"` to group and rotate the ellipses elegantly. This simplifies the code and makes it much easier to manage. I\'ve confirmed that three groups, rotated at 0, 60, and 120 degrees, give me the desired overlapping effect. Positioning glowing green balls at the ellipse vertices within the groups completes the design! Next, I will implement the turtle element.\n\n\n\n**Defining Turtle and Beaker Elements**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the turtle and beaker. I\'ve defined paths for the turtle\'s shell, flipper, head, and eye. The turtle shell will be teal and teal-dark colors. I\'m shaping the beaker\'s glass dome using an arc, including a glass effect. I\'ve designed the wave and plan to implement the fluid using curved paths and a neon green color.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will view `/src/components/Footer.tsx` to see how the logo is used in the footer."
