  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Summarizing Project Status**\n\nI\'m pleased to report a flawless build completion! My primary focus now shifts to crafting a concise summary of the project. I plan to explain the optimal method for storing account details. I\'m also considering the best structure for presenting this information, and I\'ve started by confirming the logo integration.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I have successfully integrated your official high-res **Logo.png** into the application. \n\nHere is a summary of the design implementation and an analysis of the best storage solutions for your backend:\n\n### \360\237\216\250 Logo Integration\n- **Asset Positioning**: Moved your uploaded `Logo.png` into the `/public` directory to ensure Vite serves it flawlessly across both local development and production environments.\n- **Dynamic Crop Component**: Re-engineered `TurtleRockLogo` to load the official PNG. When rendered at smaller sizes (such as the `44px` header icon or `36px` footer icon) where `hideText` is enabled, it uses CSS boundaries to elegantly zoom in on the central cute turtle and beaker dome, concealing the text ring and producing a beautiful, high-contrast avatar icon.\n\n---\n\n### \360\237\227\204\357\270\217 Where is the best place for the backend to store account details?\n\nFor full-stack modern web applications, the best practice is to separate **identity credentials** from **application-level account data**:\n\n#### 1. For Authentication & Identity: **Firebase Authentication**\n* **What to store**: Email addresses, hashed passwords, OAuth provider tokens, verification states, and unique User IDs (`uid`).\n* **Why**: It is highly secure, complies with industry security standards out-of-the-box, manages session tokens automatically, and eliminates the risk of accidentally exposing raw user passwords in application logs or database queries.\n\n#### 2. For User Profiles & Account Details: **Firebase Firestore (Cloud Firestore)**\n* **What to store**: User profiles linked to their `uid` (such as real name, username, biography, chosen avatar), user roles (e.g., *Student*, *Facilitator*, *Scientist*), mission completion histories, collected badges, daily XP progress, and active log entries.\n* **Why**:\n  * **Seamless Document Mapping**: It is a NoSQL document database, allowing you to easily map user state as flexible JSON documents.\n  * **Real-time Synchronization**: Built-in WebSockets allow your React client to listen for real-time changes to the user\'s progress or achievements instantly.\n  * **Granular Security Rules**: Firestore Security Rules allow you to define exactly who can read or write their profiles (e.g., `allow write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == userId;`), protecting account details from being edited by other users.\n  * **Offline Support**: It caches data locally automatically, meaning users can view their science logs or active achievements even when internet connectivity is spotty.\n"
    thinking_duration {
      nanos: 335105404
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
